\section{Scale, and why it is named}{
A Selection coefficient is on the \strong{latent probit scale} unless the fit says
otherwise, and the caption says which. It is not an odds ratio and not a
probability. For a quantity an applied reader can act on, use
\code{\link[=selection_effect]{selection_effect()}}, whose default is an average marginal probability
contrast rather than a coefficient.
}

\section{What is not drawn}{
Latent factors \code{U} and \code{V} are identified only up to rotation, reflection,
and scaling, so their coordinates carry no meaning of their own and are not
plotted. Only the rotation-invariant product \verb{U V'} is ever reportable, and
it is averaged over draws \strong{as a product} — averaging \code{U} and \code{V}
separately would average across each draw's arbitrary rotation and shrink
the structure toward nothing. Nothing here marks a term as significant: the
interval is drawn and the reading is yours.
}

\section{Refusals rather than approximations}{
\code{"posterior"} and \code{"trace"} need retained draws. A fit that kept none refuses
rather than drawing a normal curve built from the estimate and its standard
error — the reason to look at a posterior is that it need not be normal.
\code{"actor_effects"} draws posterior means without intervals, because the
sampler reports actor effects only as posterior means; the caption says so.
\code{"gof"} needs networks simulated from the posterior, so it refuses a
hierarchical fit, whose sampler does not simulate any, and a fit run with
\code{gof = FALSE}; \code{\link[=netis_gof]{netis_gof()}} states both refusals in full.

Chain length is reported as a count, never judged: the caption states how many
draws were retained and that Monte Carlo error scales with that number, and
leaves the adequacy question to you.
}

\section{Multi-group fits}{
Every group of a multi-group fit has its own posterior draws, actor effects
and multiplicative structure, so \code{"posterior"}, \code{"trace"}, \code{"mcmc"},
\code{"actor_effects"} and \code{"multiplicative"} take a \code{group}. On a fit with
groups estimated separately (\code{pooling = NULL}, pooled or not) they refuse
without one. On a hierarchical fit (\code{pooling = selection_pooling()})
\code{"posterior"}, \code{"trace"} and \code{"mcmc"} show the population-level draws when
\code{group} is \code{NULL} and that group's coefficient draws when it is named;
\code{"actor_effects"} and \code{"multiplicative"} need a group. A coefficient with no
population level (\code{unpooled}) has no population-level draws.

\code{"coefficients"} shows one group's rows when \code{group} is named, and otherwise
labels each per-group row with its group. A two-stage pooled fit has one row per
term and none per group, so naming a group there is refused, as is \code{"groups"}. On
a hierarchical fit a group's rows are its random and unpooled coefficients; a shared
coefficient has only its population row. \code{"heterogeneity"} reads
\code{\link[=netis_variance_components]{netis_variance_components()}}, which refuses a hierarchical fit.

\code{"gof"} is the one panel that needs no \code{group} on a multi-group fit: every
group was fitted to its own network and so has its own observed statistics
and its own simulated ones, and the panel shows them all, one row of histograms per
group and one column per statistic. Naming a group narrows it to that group.
}

\section{Latent coordinates}{

\code{U} and \code{V} are identified only up to rotation, reflection and scaling, and
each retained draw sits in its own frame, so averaging them separately would
shrink the structure toward nothing. \code{"latent"} does not do that. It takes the
posterior mean of the \strong{product} \verb{U V'}, which is identified, and decomposes
that one matrix by its singular value decomposition, scaling each side by the
root of the singular value. The inner product of a sender's point and a
receiver's point then reproduces the best rank-two approximation of the
product. The coordinates are one frame for an identified quantity: read
directions and distances from the centre, and not the axes. This construction
is this package's own display choice, not a published estimator of \code{U} and
\code{V}. \code{"multiplicative"} shows the product itself.
}

\examples{
# Cost: an amen MCMC fit, over CRAN's example budget at any usable chain length.
\donttest{
# An MCMC fit at CRAN-safe scale; a real analysis runs a far longer chain.
set.seed(1)
ids <- sprintf("n\%02d", 1:10)
nodes <- data.frame(group = 1, node = ids, x = stats::rnorm(10))
pairs <- subset(expand.grid(source = ids, target = ids,
                            stringsAsFactors = FALSE), source != target)
ties <- data.frame(group = 1, pairs,
                   tie = stats::rbinom(nrow(pairs), 1, 0.25))
nd <- netis_data(nodes, ties,
                 tie_meta = list(tie = list(zero_meaning = "measured_zero")))
fit <- fit_selection(
  tie ~ sender(x) + receiver(x), data = nd,
  nscan = 200, burn = 50, odens = 10, seed = 1
)
plot(fit)
}

}
